1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,059
Look, on New Year’s Day I will do a new thing. Even now it is about to happen.

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:11,980
This year is something to look forward to. I thought this was a New Year’s message,

3
00:00:12,039 --> 00:00:22,739
but the next day my partner—he has supported me every day for 15 years, all across Japan from Okinawa to Hokkaido four times.

4
00:00:23,359 --> 00:00:34,579
For seven months across the American continent, he supported me beside me every day—meals, water, and nights in America.

5
00:00:34,579 --> 00:00:43,600
It was nothing but salt-and-pepper, oily food. But he’s in the middle of the Texas desert,

6
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:50,539
making chilled somen. Making curry rice. Making mapo rice.

7
00:00:50,539 --> 00:01:01,299
Making oyakodon. He truly devoted himself to support—Cuba, from Santiago to Havana,

8
00:01:01,299 --> 00:01:08,159
1,000 kilometers in 26 days, walking 32 km every day, and he supported me.

9
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:20,040
Taiwan, from end to end. Last May he supported me in Taiwan too, and we walked together from Busan to Panmunjom in Korea three times.

10
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:32,780
And Shikoku around the island, and Kyushu around the island. For 15 years he has been my constant partner.

11
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:44,719
I used to have a partner named Matsuda, but I didn’t know—he was gone because of meth.

12
00:01:44,719 --> 00:01:52,079
He was 53 then, and I was 55, I think, around that.

13
00:01:52,079 --> 00:02:01,060
In life you meet all kinds of people, and some become friends—like David and Jonathan.

14
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:13,639
Building that kind of friendship takes time. I thought, “I’m done with friends,”

15
00:02:13,639 --> 00:02:24,080
and I’d go on as a lone wolf. Then at my 60th I decided to carry the cross again and walk,

16
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:30,080
and when I did in my 40s, the yakuza gathered and said, “Put out into the deep and lower your nets,”

17
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:38,360
obeying that Bible word, I set out on a journey of roadside evangelism through all 47 prefectures, and at night we held meetings at churches.

18
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:47,960
We took church people with us, and evangelized at station fronts. At the center of all 47 prefectures, over about two months,

19
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:57,979
we walked to the corners of Japan doing roadside evangelism and held meetings at night. Then pastors said, “People aren’t easily saved in places like this.”

20
00:02:57,979 --> 00:03:05,719
But God said, don’t listen to people—look at what the Bible says.

21
00:03:05,719 --> 00:03:10,780
God makes the impossible possible. Believe it and do it. When I said that,

22
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:16,159
people kept getting saved, and pastors were scratching their heads. “This shouldn’t happen,”

23
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:23,159
but it is happening. Earlier someone said that this year we should read the Word more and practice it,

24
00:03:23,159 --> 00:03:29,039
in the end it’s not our thoughts, but what God’s Word is saying to us.

25
00:03:29,039 --> 00:03:38,319
Believers are moved by God’s Word. Artists are filled with the spirit of the source of art

26
00:03:38,319 --> 00:03:44,439
and given inspiration. So like Keichi, we must treasure the Word.

27
00:03:44,439 --> 00:03:48,599
Unfold the Word and spread your own work there.

28
00:03:48,599 --> 00:03:52,840
Of course, everyone knows that this is obvious, but

29
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:58,620
your work is to express the invisible God in visible form.

30
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:05,719
And through what is visible, how much people feel the invisible God.

31
00:04:05,719 --> 00:04:14,800
That is an important evangelism method—a way to proclaim, a way to express.

32
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:24,180
In that sense, to keep the Word and express it—that is our work.

33
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:31,000
But in the Bible, “If you remain in my word, you are truly my disciples.”

34
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,319
“And you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.” So we all

35
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:46,399
are disciples if we remain in the Word. And those disciples express their master

36
00:04:46,399 --> 00:04:53,720
through the gifts given to them. That is exactly

37
00:04:53,720 --> 00:05:02,139
the Lord of artists within artists—that each of us is God’s masterpiece.

38
00:05:02,139 --> 00:05:09,060
“Look at me,” he said, showing the Christ within me. Paul said that, but

39
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:17,079
now the inspiration in my heart is proof that I was created by God and kept alive.

40
00:05:17,079 --> 00:05:24,139
Through that proof, people see it and wonder if there is One who made them.

41
00:05:24,139 --> 00:05:28,879
And as this person expresses, maybe I can express something too.

42
00:05:28,879 --> 00:05:36,720
The sensitivity of the Holy Spirit awakens. So in that sense, my evangelism too

43
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:41,839
is an artist drawing the universal with a brush of words on the canvas of your heart.

44
00:05:41,839 --> 00:05:52,519
In the end, words alone are not enough. Words must be expressed in our work.

45
00:05:52,519 --> 00:05:58,500
Showing what you say, making others do it—competing through your work.

46
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:07,899
Your work is proof of your existence, and your work is proof of the One who made you a masterpiece.

47
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:14,439
That is to show His glory. So in that sense, we are all artists.

48
00:06:14,439 --> 00:06:22,300
Those created and kept alive by God show His glory—that He is an amazing One.

49
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:32,860
Through ourselves and through our works, we express God’s Word with action—this is our work.

50
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:40,199
Doing that work, we then led the yakuza in a cross march.

51
00:06:40,199 --> 00:06:45,819
I led them, and everyone gathered. So when you set your heart and take a step,

52
00:06:45,819 --> 00:06:52,379
it’s mysterious, like the world of Momotaro: “Give me one of your millet dumplings.”

53
00:06:52,379 --> 00:07:03,620
When your work touches people’s hearts, even if you’re not trying to be liked or accepted,

54
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:09,639
if you have the feeling that you are accepted and sent by God,

55
00:07:09,639 --> 00:07:18,160
that gets conveyed: “I want to support you,” “I’m captivated by your painting and want to buy it,”

56
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:24,680
“I want to display it at home.” Those reactions arise. No need to flatter people.

57
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:33,000
No need to worry about money. You might, but if you put God’s kingdom and righteousness first,

58
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:40,019
all that you need will be added and given to you. So I’ve worked as a devotee, but

59
00:07:40,019 --> 00:07:46,519
I’m also trying to teach George and Lisa—don’t flatter money, don’t flatter people.

60
00:07:46,519 --> 00:07:56,199
Don’t fawn on the rich or the honorable. Everyone is a priest. Each person is God’s masterpiece.

61
00:07:56,199 --> 00:08:05,720
I’ve lived 40 years like that, and I’m continually supported. Money is necessary, but

62
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:14,399
when you accept God and have faith, what you need will be added and given.

63
00:08:14,399 --> 00:08:20,019
As long as you worry about money, you paint cheap pictures and make cheap works.

64
00:08:20,019 --> 00:08:25,620
When you stop worrying about money and focus on expressing God’s glory,

65
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:30,620
what you need will be given—always follows. Right, Keichi?

66
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:38,700
It might sound like I’m threatening you, but I really think it’s true. It really is.

67
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:48,879
So for those who draw and express—me too—don’t flatter, don’t butter up. Look to God,

68
00:08:48,879 --> 00:08:54,480
listen to His word, commune with Him, and He gives inspiration,

69
00:08:54,480 --> 00:09:02,879
letting you express what can only be expressed at this moment. That is certain.

70
00:09:02,879 --> 00:09:16,860
My partner once came to me; his wife is Christian, and he is a former Muslim.

71
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:25,759
He’s been building schools for 40 years for poor children in Bangladesh—250 schools.

72
00:09:25,759 --> 00:09:32,600
He started with volunteer work and formed an NPO,

73
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:40,139
but it’s not for his own profit. He lives by managing his pension with his wife.

74
00:09:40,139 --> 00:09:45,960
He’s 76, two years older than me. He passed away recently, and his wife is the same age.

75
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:59,159
Bangladesh is said to be one of the poorest countries in the world. He once worked in a foundry there.

76
00:09:59,159 --> 00:10:06,820
A foundry is a factory with chimneys where you melt metal and sweat while working.

77
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:14,879
One day a Bangladeshi student came to work there. “Where’s your country?” “Bangladesh.”

78
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:20,059
“What kind of place?” “You’ll understand better if you come.”

79
00:10:20,059 --> 00:10:25,720
He said, “Then I’ll go,” and he went that summer. He saw Bangladesh’s poverty,

80
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:33,539
and that girls can’t get education—sold in their teens to become prostitutes.

81
00:10:33,539 --> 00:10:39,279
Seeing that, he thought, “How sad,” and for those poor children,

82
00:10:39,279 --> 00:10:46,240
especially girls, he got a vision to build schools and started,

83
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:52,080
and various companies began supporting him, and he has built 250 schools.

84
00:10:52,080 --> 00:11:01,559
For 40 years he has been cherished in Bangladesh. His work is truly about coming alongside.

85
00:11:01,559 --> 00:11:08,240
Blessed are the merciful, for they will receive mercy. That’s Jesus’ words.

86
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:16,519
In other words, blessed are the compassionate, for they will receive compassion.

87
00:11:16,519 --> 00:11:23,200
Blessed are those with mercy, for they will receive mercy, it says, but

88
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:31,259
in the end our God is the One who comes alongside. He became Muslim

89
00:11:31,259 --> 00:11:39,000
because “when in Rome,” in a Muslim country. One day his wife came to Bangladesh.

90
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:48,399
She had divorced someone within a month because it didn’t work out. She had been bullied from childhood,

91
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:58,620
but one day at a missionary’s Sunday school, the missionary really came alongside her,

92
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:07,159
and she came to believe in Jesus. After she believed in Jesus, her wounds were healed,

93
00:12:07,159 --> 00:12:15,340
and she connected with Jesus. She’s clumsy and eccentric, but she has strong faith.

94
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:28,440
When she went to Bangladesh, he tried to introduce her to a Bangladeshi student.

95
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:38,059
“He’s a good person.” “No, I won’t marry a Muslim. I’ll only marry a Christian.”

96
00:12:38,059 --> 00:12:44,840
He thought, “There’s someone like that in this day and age.” Then he brazenly asked, “How about me?”

97
00:12:44,840 --> 00:12:52,779
He was already once divorced. Over the long relationship, he met his wife but didn’t have children.

98
00:12:52,779 --> 00:12:56,799
When he asked, “How about me?” she said, “If you become Christian, we can marry.”

99
00:12:56,799 --> 00:13:02,759
They were both eccentric. He said, “Then I’ll become Christian,” declaring it before he was.

100
00:13:02,759 --> 00:13:12,480
So they got married. He hates fitting into boxes, so when he went to church with her,

101
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:22,100
they had a baptism preparation class. He thought, “Why make it so difficult?”

102
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:26,259
“If he wants baptism, just do it right away.”

103
00:13:26,259 --> 00:13:33,980
They put it to a board meeting, like a religious court. Even after a date was set, it got postponed,

104
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:41,419
so he snapped and stormed the church in anger. “What kind of Christ is that?”

105
00:13:41,419 --> 00:13:48,259
“Pastor, don’t just talk—show what you say with action!”

106
00:13:48,259 --> 00:13:53,960
He’s that kind of guy, so the pastor was intimidated.

107
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:59,059
When it came time for his funeral,

108
00:13:59,059 --> 00:14:12,039
his wife asked the pastor of her church, but she wanted me to give a message since I knew Bangra. The pastor is bound by forms,

109
00:14:12,039 --> 00:14:21,559
and said he had to do it his way. So we split it: the pastor did the required funeral,

110
00:14:21,559 --> 00:14:27,220
and afterward I gave a message

111
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:34,259
to the 150 people present. In his message, the pastor said,

112
00:14:34,259 --> 00:14:43,220
“I only met Mr. Bangra twice. The one time he said, ‘Pastor, you have to move more,’

113
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:50,919
‘Just talking isn’t enough. Show what you say with action,’ he told me.” He met him only twice.

114
00:14:50,919 --> 00:15:00,959
I spent 15 years with him, sometimes traveling together for nearly half a year. In the morning we’d wake up and praise together,

115
00:15:00,959 --> 00:15:06,399
and I’d give him messages. God—he was right-brained,

116
00:15:06,399 --> 00:15:12,819
so instead of hard-to-read text, there were about 47 parables,

117
00:15:12,819 --> 00:15:18,699
so I’d use those parables and give him images.

118
00:15:18,699 --> 00:15:26,839
He had trouble reading the Bible, so he read it from manga-like picture books for children.

119
00:15:26,839 --> 00:15:32,299
And every time he read it, he’d say, “Jesus is amazing,” and so on.

120
00:15:32,299 --> 00:15:40,480
His wife and he went with me to Israel and both got baptized in the Jordan River.

121
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:50,620
He’s the type who acts by feeling rather than words. So even before becoming Christian, I said, “Bangra, you’re amazing.”

122
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:58,620
In the Bible, Jesus says, “Care for widows and orphans.” That’s the church’s mission.

123
00:15:58,620 --> 00:16:05,000
But even before understanding the Bible, even when you said you were Muslim,

124
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:15,159
you already cared for children and built schools. As someone created by God,

125
00:16:15,159 --> 00:16:22,620
you were unconsciously, consciously doing God’s will. “That’s wonderful,” I often told him.

126
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:31,279
What you’ve done for 40 years—how many times a year you go to Bangladesh. Work that doesn’t make money,

127
00:16:31,279 --> 00:16:38,559
not for money. Because the children are pitiful, you practice love.

128
00:16:38,559 --> 00:16:46,500
One time he sent me a photo. The child was already grown, but

129
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:51,959
it was a child with a facial deformity—eyes here, mouth there.

130
00:16:51,959 --> 00:17:00,220
Just seeing it made you want to say, “Don’t send me that.” “Go meet this child,” he said.

131
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:08,099
When he came back he said, “Why couldn’t I hug that child?”

132
00:17:08,099 --> 00:17:16,039
He regretted it. “Why are we born like this?” We don’t know.

133
00:17:16,039 --> 00:17:22,240
People aren’t perfect; we’re sinners, and someday we’ll die,

134
00:17:22,240 --> 00:17:31,240
in the end we live apart from God, turning away from His blessings in many ways.

135
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:36,179
Through the seeds we sow and the injustices of the world,

136
00:17:36,179 --> 00:17:48,039
we have a hand in making today’s world. But God says He will show His glory through each person,

137
00:17:48,039 --> 00:17:58,579
beyond our understanding. When you say someone is beautiful, you define who is beautiful,

138
00:17:58,579 --> 00:18:06,440
yet the girl Bangra couldn’t hug is beautiful before God.

139
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:19,919
We can’t see that as beautiful. That’s the built beauty. But on the other hand, forgetting that we are God’s masterpieces,

140
00:18:19,919 --> 00:18:29,559
humans destroy the image God created. And the broken us view others

141
00:18:29,559 --> 00:18:38,659
through broken values. When we met Ban-chan,

142
00:18:38,659 --> 00:18:47,019
sometimes people came when there were many, but crossing the American continent,

143
00:18:47,019 --> 00:18:55,059
at first people came, but in the middle of the desert no one comes—just me and Bangra at the start and end.

144
00:18:55,059 --> 00:19:04,159
Even if he couldn’t speak the language, he communicates with people by heart. At restaurants,

145
00:19:04,159 --> 00:19:09,359
he’d say, “Hey miss, give me this.” “She won’t understand ‘miss,’”

146
00:19:09,359 --> 00:19:13,519
“Ask if they have alcohol.” “Miss, do you have alcohol?”

147
00:19:13,519 --> 00:19:20,140
Just like that—and it works. And the big American waitress ladies

148
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:28,900
say, “Oh, he’s so cute.” He’s like a little mascot, short.

149
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:36,440
“I want to take him home.” And he’d say arrogantly, “Arthur, I’m popular in America.”

150
00:19:36,440 --> 00:19:44,900
Then one time, about two years ago, he said, “Arthur, I thought about it.”

151
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:53,700
“When your friend Chu died, I saw your sad face, and I thought,”

152
00:19:53,700 --> 00:20:04,059
“After I send Arthur and my wife Keiko to heaven, then I’ll die.”

153
00:20:04,059 --> 00:20:10,679
“Hold on,” I said. “You’re trying to outlive me? I think I’ll outlive you.”

154
00:20:10,679 --> 00:20:17,419
“No, when Arthur dies, in front of everyone, I know Arthur the best,”

155
00:20:17,419 --> 00:20:24,519
“people only see Arthur’s glamorous side, but I’ve seen his hard times.

156
00:20:24,519 --> 00:20:30,839
I want to talk.” “Don’t decide that yourself.”

157
00:20:30,839 --> 00:20:39,299
“I’ll talk at your funeral,” I said. “Don’t think you’ll go first.” We talked like that,

158
00:20:39,299 --> 00:20:46,839
but in the end he said, “I’ll send Keiko and Arthur first,” but

159
00:20:46,839 --> 00:20:51,740
you lied—you went first. That’s how it ended up feeling.

160
00:20:51,740 --> 00:20:59,900
That’s the attachment. “I will do a new thing. Even now it is about to happen.”

161
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:05,400
If you take the Bible words positively, you wonder what will happen this year.

162
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:14,339
Is Bangra dying already the new thing? But there’s a message in it.

163
00:21:14,339 --> 00:21:18,740
A new thing is not the new thing we think of,

164
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:27,339
but the new thing God wants to teach us. People make plans, but the Lord accomplishes them,

165
00:21:27,339 --> 00:21:36,140
as the Bible says. “For everything under heaven there is a season, and a time for every activity.”

166
00:21:36,140 --> 00:21:42,720
“A time to be born, and a time to die.” So Bangra’s death is

167
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:47,619
not just about Bangra. We all die too, sorry to say.

168
00:21:47,619 --> 00:21:55,099
This is destiny. But God is the One who does new things for people like us.

169
00:21:55,099 --> 00:22:00,640
God’s new things are eternally new.

170
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:10,059
He does new things that even overcome death. Death cannot stand against God’s new work.

171
00:22:10,059 --> 00:22:19,380
He is the One who raises the dead. In the Bible, when we die and sleep,

172
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:27,380
and the Lord returns to this earth again, with one voice we will all awaken and be given new bodies—

173
00:22:27,380 --> 00:22:36,599
that is our hope. But you who paint must face the reality of death

174
00:22:36,599 --> 00:22:48,240
and the reality of being kept alive, and beyond that, the truth that even death is resurrected,

175
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:54,859
and whether you express that as artists is what is asked. Death is not the end.

176
00:22:54,859 --> 00:23:02,899
Death is like passing through a tunnel. But whether you have that certainty or not

177
00:23:02,899 --> 00:23:08,740
shows up in how you live. “What fills the heart shows on the face,” it says, but

178
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:18,059
I’ve held many funerals, and when I saw Bangra’s face,

179
00:23:18,059 --> 00:23:28,820
I thought, “I’ve never seen a face smiling like that.” He was smiling. And his beard was neatly trimmed.

180
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:39,079
It used to be messy, but his complexion was good. I asked, “Which funeral home did you use for this makeup?”

181
00:23:39,079 --> 00:23:46,899
She said, “We didn’t hire a funeral home.” They don’t hire one. If you do, you have to leave everything to them.

182
00:23:46,899 --> 00:23:54,960
Bangra helped with funerals. When someone died in a hospital, he’d rush over

183
00:23:54,960 --> 00:24:04,359
and put the body in his “Bangra car,” a car he painted himself. He’d buy a brand-new car,

184
00:24:04,359 --> 00:24:12,880
strip all the paint with sandpaper, then say, “Arthur, could you paint my car?”

185
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:17,960
He’d say, “Express all your feelings there,” so it’s art.

186
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:24,579
Like “art is an explosion,” we’d paint hearts, arrows,

187
00:24:24,579 --> 00:24:27,899
“I love you,” and “Jesus loves you,” all kinds of things.

188
00:24:27,899 --> 00:24:34,299
With the inspiration of the moment, everyone gathered and painted with different paints.

189
00:24:34,299 --> 00:24:45,359
His wife also drove. When she bought a new car, she’d always have her husband sand it down first,

190
00:24:45,359 --> 00:24:52,099
then everyone would paint it—otherwise she’d feel bad driving it.

191
00:24:52,099 --> 00:24:59,799
That’s the kind of person he was. So we all painted together—that was the fun. Then we drove it.

192
00:24:59,799 --> 00:25:08,380
The Bangra car—the first time it came to pick me up in Kagoshima, I rode it,

193
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:15,960
and this time we put his coffin on it and went to the crematorium. Normally you use a hearse.

194
00:25:15,960 --> 00:25:23,399
When we arrived, the crematorium staff were in black suits waiting. “Huh?” We went in that.

195
00:25:23,399 --> 00:25:39,579
We went in that. His coffin was custom made. He used to make coffins himself, buying wood online and fitting the size,

196
00:25:39,579 --> 00:25:47,380
and then everyone would doodle and paint on them. Everyone painted—cat lovers painted cats,

197
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:53,380
and if the deceased liked cats, they’d paint cats. Then we’d carry that coffin.

198
00:25:53,380 --> 00:26:03,000
For him, since he always said “Bangra,” the Bangladesh flag is like Japan’s, with red in the center.

199
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:11,820
Outside is white, but Bangladesh is green. Red and green. A two-tone red and green coffin,

200
00:26:11,820 --> 00:26:19,779
made by my acquaintance Ken, a carpenter. It was made so that just this part opens,

201
00:26:19,779 --> 00:26:30,220
so you can see his face. His face looked very youthful, and his beard was trimmed.

202
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:35,079
I asked, “Did you use a makeup artist?” His wife said, “No, it’s just like this.”

203
00:26:35,079 --> 00:26:48,759
“Such a beautiful face, and the beard is trimmed.” She said, “From the 23rd to the 30th, at year’s end, we went to Cebu,

204
00:26:48,759 --> 00:26:56,679
and had a week’s vacation.” Bangra loved his wife, so he always took her on a hot-spring trip at year’s end.

205
00:26:56,679 --> 00:27:02,000
This time they went to Cebu. He sent me about 20 photos.

206
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:08,980
They were doing skits with this woman. I thought this duo would be a hit at Yoshimoto Kogyo,

207
00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:16,679
but all the photos were of them eating. “Keiko is eating a lot again,” he sent.

208
00:27:16,679 --> 00:27:25,919
On the last day, he went to a salon to have his beard trimmed,

209
00:27:25,919 --> 00:27:30,940
and the stylist was gay, and he had his beard trimmed by him.

210
00:27:30,940 --> 00:27:36,320
So from my perspective, he came back on the 31st and died the next day. So

211
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:45,320
maybe he unconsciously sought a makeup artist, but he came back with a tidy face.

212
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:49,759
When we brought this to the crematorium,

213
00:27:49,759 --> 00:28:02,700
he used blue tarps for earthquake repairs, so he wrapped this coffin in blue tarps and carried it.

214
00:28:02,700 --> 00:28:19,679
The people there were stunned, but he was a legend. My partner Goto asked, “Do people bring coffins like this?” “Maybe once a year.”

215
00:28:19,679 --> 00:28:25,679
“Is it mostly related to Mr. Bangra?” “I can’t say.”

216
00:28:25,679 --> 00:28:30,899
Bangra made all kinds of coffins and brought them in his own car.

217
00:28:30,899 --> 00:28:42,539
So it’s not straightforward. He was a right-brained artist. The car too—that’s his—

218
00:28:42,539 --> 00:28:51,200
he went in with this. This footage is often shown. With this, everyone knows he’s from Bangladesh,

219
00:28:51,200 --> 00:28:57,720
and everyone sat to thank Bangra—150 people. In this small rural town, everyone sat.

220
00:28:57,720 --> 00:29:09,659
We put this in and bring it to the crematorium, but he asked everyone to sign the coffin at the end.

221
00:29:09,659 --> 00:29:17,619
Not on paper, but with colored markers. The funeral staff said, “Please do that further inside,”

222
00:29:17,619 --> 00:29:22,819
because people can’t see it. They’re the type who say, “Respect the remains.”

223
00:29:22,819 --> 00:29:27,859
We respect the remains, but we want to do what the deceased wanted.

224
00:29:27,859 --> 00:29:31,140
The remains are telling us, “Do it like this.”

225
00:29:31,140 --> 00:29:35,259
Before the crematorium, we all used markers: “Thank you, Bangra.”

226
00:29:35,259 --> 00:29:39,799
His wife drew caricatures of herself and Bangra on the coffin.

227
00:29:39,799 --> 00:29:48,500
And we carried it to the furnace. While people were signing, the latch on the lid

228
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:54,940
fell off with a clank. “Just leave it,” kind of feeling.

229
00:29:54,940 --> 00:30:01,159
That was the vibe of the funeral.

230
00:30:01,159 --> 00:30:08,559
But that’s it—if you leave it to a funeral company, it becomes a conveyor-belt funeral.

231
00:30:08,559 --> 00:30:17,779
No originality. You just follow their lead. And when you pick up the bones they say, “This is the Adam’s apple,”

232
00:30:17,779 --> 00:30:28,259
and say irrelevant things. “We’re not about Buddha.” That becomes the world’s norm.

233
00:30:28,259 --> 00:30:35,880
In painting, you can only do things within those frames. That’s the world’s norm, social appearance.

234
00:30:35,880 --> 00:30:41,159
But when you’re an artist—both Bangra and I are artists.

235
00:30:41,159 --> 00:30:50,200
“Arthur, everyone looks at my car,” he said. Of course they do—more than a Rolls or a Benz.

236
00:30:50,200 --> 00:31:03,559
It stands out the most. But you don’t see that on the road. And no one strips the original paint with sandpaper

237
00:31:03,559 --> 00:31:09,659
and then paints it. But there’s someone who does.

238
00:31:09,659 --> 00:31:20,440
So Bangra received the word “I will do a new thing,” and two days later he died.

239
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:31,440
Could you put up today’s Bible verse? The message: “A year when new things happen.”

240
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:37,759
I said that on day one, but something I didn’t expect happened.

241
00:31:37,759 --> 00:31:48,299
Isaiah 43:18–21: “Do not remember the former things, nor consider the things of old.”

242
00:31:48,299 --> 00:31:57,159
“Behold, I will do a new thing.” George, do it after you hear my words. “Behold, I will do a new thing.”

243
00:31:57,159 --> 00:32:02,639
“Now it is about to happen; do you not know it?”

244
00:32:02,639 --> 00:32:11,380
“Indeed I will make a way in the wilderness, and rivers in the desert.” Verse 20: “The beasts of the field, jackals and ostriches,”

245
00:32:11,380 --> 00:32:20,059
there are paintings of animals, lizards, lions, horses. “The beasts of the field, jackals and ostriches,”

246
00:32:20,059 --> 00:32:27,679
“will honor me, because I provide water in the wilderness and rivers in the desert, to give drink to my people,”

247
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:37,539
“my chosen ones. The people I formed for myself will declare my praise.” You are

248
00:32:37,539 --> 00:32:49,819
a people created by God. To proclaim His glory, His honor, we are chosen and

249
00:32:49,819 --> 00:32:56,779
set up. Can you go back again? There is a famous

250
00:32:56,779 --> 00:33:02,659
British historian named Arnold Toynbee.

251
00:33:02,659 --> 00:33:12,819
He said this. The historian said…are we okay? They’re surprised by “a new thing” too.

252
00:33:12,819 --> 00:33:30,599
Arnold Toynbee said, “The very existence of the Jewish people today is a miracle.”

253
00:33:30,599 --> 00:33:39,000
The fact that the Jewish people of Israel still exist today is a miracle.

254
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:51,119
This was written in Isaiah: “Do not remember the former things,

255
00:33:51,119 --> 00:34:01,019
nor consider the things of old.” The Israelites have lost their nation three times.

256
00:34:01,019 --> 00:34:13,539
Yet they still have a nation. Losing it three times and still having one is something other nations can hardly imagine.

257
00:34:13,539 --> 00:34:23,460
But through this nation, God says, “You are no different from Israel.”

258
00:34:23,460 --> 00:34:32,659
Through Israel, He teaches us how people created by God lose sight of Him and live turning away,

259
00:34:32,659 --> 00:34:37,519
through the nation of Israel.

260
00:34:37,519 --> 00:34:44,839
Even though Israel lost its nation three times, it still exists—there is God’s mercy and providence.

261
00:34:44,839 --> 00:34:51,400
That means God’s mercy and providence are for us too.

262
00:34:51,400 --> 00:35:00,940
If you think of Israel losing its nation, anyone can understand. Ask Keichi and he’ll know.

263
00:35:00,940 --> 00:35:15,079
In Egypt, the Israelites were slaves for 430 years. That was from 1870 BC,

264
00:35:15,079 --> 00:35:26,539
to 1440 BC. For 430 years they were slaves in Egypt without a nation. There God

265
00:35:26,539 --> 00:35:37,619
was born in Egypt where all boys under two were killed, and miraculously when he was set afloat on the Nile,

266
00:35:37,619 --> 00:35:45,000
the queen saw him. She found Moses so adorable that she decided to raise him,

267
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:57,300
ultimately letting his family raise him and giving him elite education. And when he

268
00:35:57,300 --> 00:36:04,839
became an adult and went out, he saw his people being oppressed by Egyptians,

269
00:36:04,839 --> 00:36:13,219
and he killed someone. Then he fled to Midian. Did his elite education

270
00:36:13,219 --> 00:36:19,839
make him think he could lead, or was he immature?

271
00:36:19,839 --> 00:36:28,960
Did he not yet know he needed God’s training? In the end he reaped what he sowed,

272
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:37,940
fled to Midian, met Jethro’s daughter Zipporah, and shepherded under Jethro.

273
00:36:37,940 --> 00:36:47,800
For 40 years. One day he saw a burning bush. As he drew near, he heard God’s voice.

274
00:36:47,800 --> 00:36:59,000
“I have seen the suffering of my people. I know their pain. I have heard it. I send you.”

275
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:10,260
He had killed someone and fled, and now as a shepherd, he learned what it means to be a sheep,

276
00:37:10,260 --> 00:37:18,839
and how God is the Shepherd. Shepherds are raised by the sheep.

277
00:37:18,839 --> 00:37:27,900
Even the woman who is caring for her grandchild now learns through the grandchild,

278
00:37:27,900 --> 00:37:34,359
through that pure grandchild, what a human is, what she is. So God gives us partners,

279
00:37:34,359 --> 00:37:42,579
and through partners we see ourselves. So God becomes our partner—you and me—

280
00:37:42,579 --> 00:37:52,219
and through Him we come to know ourselves. So “Come to me, all who are weary.”

281
00:37:52,219 --> 00:37:59,880
“Be yoked to me.” Jesus wants to nurture us in relationship with Him.

282
00:37:59,880 --> 00:38:10,400
Things keep dropping today. Maybe a different force of interference is at work. I don’t think so, though.

283
00:38:10,400 --> 00:38:19,839
So in that sense, we really need partners.

284
00:38:19,839 --> 00:38:26,219
When someone like Mutsumi reaches out to Keiichi,

285
00:38:26,219 --> 00:38:37,440
Keiichi says, “I’ll make more work this year.” So we need each other.

286
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:44,280
We need each other, and loving one another is the proof that God is with us.

287
00:38:44,280 --> 00:38:52,940
So Moses learned that from God, and by faith rescued the Israelites,

288
00:38:52,940 --> 00:39:01,480
crossed the Red Sea, and went to Canaan—the Exodus from 430 years of slavery.

289
00:39:01,480 --> 00:39:08,819
There is that history of deliverance from Egypt. After that they worshiped God,

290
00:39:08,819 --> 00:39:15,380
but by adopting idolatry and other things, they became captives in the Babylonian Empire.

291
00:39:15,380 --> 00:39:21,440
Under King Nebuchadnezzar. Daniel’s book is famous,

292
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:26,940
and Daniel became someone like the king’s right hand there,

293
00:39:26,940 --> 00:39:39,260
but they were slaves for 70 years. Then in AD 70 they were occupied by the Roman Empire,

294
00:39:39,260 --> 00:39:51,319
Israel was destroyed and scattered. And in May 1948 they were able to rebuild the nation of Israel.

295
00:39:51,319 --> 00:40:01,280
Three times in this long history. Why is this people special? Their identity

296
00:40:01,280 --> 00:40:09,839
is believing in the Creator God and not other gods or idols—that is in their foundational teaching,

297
00:40:09,839 --> 00:40:15,319
and “Be fruitful and multiply and fill the earth,” passing on what they were taught,

298
00:40:15,319 --> 00:40:22,519
their culture and civilization. Based on the Bible’s word,

299
00:40:22,519 --> 00:40:31,859
though interpretation often goes astray. Then Jesus came, and

300
00:40:31,859 --> 00:40:40,800
“I will do a new thing,” and what Israel could not do,

301
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:48,500
now Jesus is the firstfruits of Israel, and those who believe in Jesus are Israel.

302
00:40:48,500 --> 00:40:56,599
As Abraham’s descendants, the role comes to us, to preach the gospel to all the world,

303
00:40:56,599 --> 00:41:03,599
to proclaim it to the world. Christianity has the largest population among world religions.

304
00:41:03,599 --> 00:41:14,339
So here too, no matter how much you deny it, if we live by faith in the Bible’s word,

305
00:41:14,339 --> 00:41:23,099
God, though we are imperfect, will not abandon us, the perfect One will remake us,

306
00:41:23,099 --> 00:41:29,579
and continually do new things. Therefore, in “Behold, I will do a new thing,”

307
00:41:29,579 --> 00:41:39,300
before that is “Do not remember the former things, nor consider the things of old,” meaning

308
00:41:39,300 --> 00:41:45,179
don’t sit back on Israel’s history. You did the Exodus and celebrated Passover,

309
00:41:45,179 --> 00:41:50,420
celebrations are fine, but don’t sit back on God’s works,

310
00:41:50,420 --> 00:41:56,420
I am doing a new thing now. Isaiah especially prophesied that.

311
00:41:56,420 --> 00:42:03,179
He prophesied they would be taken captive by Babylon, but would be delivered.

312
00:42:03,179 --> 00:42:10,719
That was toward our era. Babylon—those who oppose God—

313
00:42:10,719 --> 00:42:27,440
may rule and enslave us, but I will rescue you from there. I think it’s a prophecy leading to Jesus’ return. That makes sense. “Do not remember the former things, nor consider the things of old.”

314
00:42:27,440 --> 00:42:32,199
We don’t sit back on what we did in the past.

315
00:42:32,199 --> 00:42:39,319
Of course, Keichi doesn’t sit back. New works—art is to challenge the impossible

316
00:42:39,319 --> 00:42:43,859
and overcome it to create a new reality—Picasso said that.

317
00:42:43,859 --> 00:42:51,639
So we constantly create new realities. When you take a bath, dead skin washes off,

318
00:42:51,639 --> 00:42:59,940
dead skin falls away and new skin is born. We are made that way,

319
00:42:59,940 --> 00:43:07,859
so we create new works. Once one takes form, new things are born from it.

320
00:43:07,859 --> 00:43:15,800
God constantly does new things. We can’t see it now, but God promises it.

321
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:22,159
So don’t cling to past achievements or glory.

322
00:43:22,159 --> 00:43:30,199
The old has passed; behold, all things have become new. God continually does new things.

323
00:43:30,199 --> 00:43:39,139
With that faith, take a step and express it in action. “Behold, I will do a new thing,

324
00:43:39,139 --> 00:43:47,019
now it is about to happen. Do you not know it? Know it. Indeed, I will make a way in the wilderness,

325
00:43:47,019 --> 00:43:52,579
there is no road in the wilderness. But God says He will make a way in the wilderness.

326
00:43:52,579 --> 00:44:01,960
Jesus Himself says, “I am the way.” And “He restores my soul and leads me in paths of righteousness for His name’s sake.”

327
00:44:01,960 --> 00:44:11,000
So He prepares a way where there is no way. He provides a way of escape for those who suffer.

328
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:18,000
“I will make a way in the wilderness, rivers in the desert.”

329
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:28,579
There are no rivers in the desert. He makes rivers where there are none. Verse 20: “The beasts of the field, jackals and ostriches, will honor me.”

330
00:44:28,579 --> 00:44:34,000
We can say they honor Him by drinking this water. When they drink it,

331
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:42,739
they rejoice and worship God who made them. Jackals, beasts of the field, ostriches—

332
00:44:42,739 --> 00:44:50,119
in Levitical terms, they are unclean animals. But the unclean animals represent unclean us,

333
00:44:50,119 --> 00:44:56,319
sinful us. Part of it is that when humans turn away from God, they become like beasts.

334
00:44:56,319 --> 00:45:05,019
Ostriches trample their own eggs. Jackals steal prey caught by other animals.

336
00:45:05,019 --> 00:45:10,380
So humans take what others have done—that’s an aspect

337
00:45:10,380 --> 00:45:18,539
even worse than beasts. But the beasts of the field, the jackals and the ostriches will honor me.

338
00:45:18,539 --> 00:45:27,079
“Because I provide water in the wilderness and rivers in the desert, to give drink to my people, my chosen ones.”

339
00:45:27,079 --> 00:45:32,819
So everyone, God will refresh you. “Let the thirsty come to me and drink.

340
00:45:32,819 --> 00:45:37,920
Whoever believes in me, from the depths of their thirsty heart, living water will flow.”

341
00:45:37,920 --> 00:45:46,199
God has already placed a spring in the depths of your heart. He will make water spring up

342
00:45:46,199 --> 00:45:53,519
from the depths of your dry heart. God’s river is full of water.

343
00:45:53,519 --> 00:46:01,719
Entering this river, all who were dead live. Baptism represents that too.

344
00:46:01,719 --> 00:46:10,339
“The people I formed for myself will declare my praise. I give drink to my chosen ones.”

345
00:46:10,339 --> 00:46:17,639
“The people I formed for myself will declare my praise.” We are chosen.

346
00:46:17,639 --> 00:46:25,619
“I chose you and appointed you that you should go and bear much fruit,” that is, God’s glory,

347
00:46:25,619 --> 00:46:33,780
to show the glory of being connected to me. It’s intense, baby. A grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies,

348
00:46:33,780 --> 00:46:38,299
then it bears much fruit. Because Jesus Christ died and rose on the cross,

349
00:46:38,299 --> 00:46:48,119
we can bear much fruit. So a new thing happens. Bangra died.

350
00:46:48,119 --> 00:46:56,359
No one knows his achievements. When I gave a message in front of his coffin,

351
00:46:56,359 --> 00:47:01,239
if something happened, Bangra would go the next day. He never stayed still.

352
00:47:01,239 --> 00:47:09,119
He devoted his life to hospitality. Even when people couldn’t eat, he kept cooking.

353
00:47:09,119 --> 00:47:16,659
Even in America, my mother came to see me, and Bangra said, “I’ll treat you to Bangra curry,”

354
00:47:16,659 --> 00:47:28,219
and he reheated the curry on a stove in the restroom, stirring it with a big rice paddle.

355
00:47:28,219 --> 00:47:38,679
My mother happened to see him licking the spoon and placing it by the toilet,

356
00:47:38,679 --> 00:47:47,319
and she said, “Masayuki,”—my name is “Ma-chan”—“Ma-chan, Ban-chan puts that spoon by the toilet,

357
00:47:47,319 --> 00:48:00,679
and then stirs and licks again.” The first time I experienced Bangra’s support, I had diarrhea for 21 days—three weeks.

358
00:48:00,679 --> 00:48:05,559
He came to support me—did he come to infect me?

359
00:48:05,559 --> 00:48:13,839
But after receiving the baptism of Bangra’s germs, I stopped fearing germs. I always kept in my heart the words,

360
00:48:13,839 --> 00:48:20,339
“Even if bitten by a poisonous snake, you will not die.” Bangra doesn’t wash his hands, blows his nose, and makes my sandwich.

361
00:48:20,339 --> 00:48:29,519
He cuts lettuce with a knife he dropped on the roadside. And I don’t worry and eat it.

362
00:48:29,519 --> 00:48:38,380
When I travel with Bangra, hygiene is zero. Zero—but I trust God.

363
00:48:38,380 --> 00:48:44,799
So when COVID came, I wasn’t scared at all. I had Bangra’s immunity. Because Bangra

364
00:48:44,799 --> 00:48:48,159
doesn’t get COVID. COVID runs away from Bangra.

365
00:48:48,159 --> 00:48:57,299
COVID doesn’t want Bangra’s germs and runs away. So I tell this story and everyone laughs.

366
00:48:57,299 --> 00:49:04,619
Bangra wants everyone to laugh. He doesn’t want them to cry.

367
00:49:04,619 --> 00:49:11,299
“Weeping may stay for the night, but rejoicing comes in the morning.”

368
00:49:11,299 --> 00:49:18,679
Bangra wants you to rejoice. He says, “We’ll meet again.” You don’t notice,

369
00:49:18,679 --> 00:49:26,799
Bangra is sleeping here. “I’ve never seen Bangra this quiet,” pointing at Bangra.

370
00:49:26,799 --> 00:49:37,139
But only when Bangra is quiet do his words and actions resonate with us now.

371
00:49:37,139 --> 00:49:47,079
Silence can exist without words. Silence cannot exist without words. Only in silence do we understand.

372
00:49:47,079 --> 00:49:54,539
Only when he says nothing do we understand how much he did,

373
00:49:54,539 --> 00:50:02,579
how many famous words he spoke. While alive people don’t notice, but after death they do.

374
00:50:03,359 --> 00:50:09,659
A grain of wheat fell into the earth and died, and how much fruit it left in many hearts.

375
00:50:09,659 --> 00:50:18,199
His death testifies to his life. May we also say, “I am the resurrection and the life.

376
00:50:18,199 --> 00:50:22,920
Whoever believes in me will live even if they die. Whoever lives and believes in me will never die.”

377
00:50:22,920 --> 00:50:30,819
Holding this Lord of resurrection in our hearts, we too die to self and live to Christ.

378
00:50:30,819 --> 00:50:38,579
In that repeated process, as artists we express the glory of Jesus Christ,

379
00:50:38,579 --> 00:50:45,179
and may each of us become an artist. So stop lamenting what you can’t do.

380
00:50:45,179 --> 00:50:52,659
Let’s do what we can. Don’t grumble about what you can’t do—express what you can and rejoice.

381
00:50:52,659 --> 00:51:01,339
Don’t do nothing. There is something you can do. It is proof that each of you is kept alive.

382
00:51:01,339 --> 00:51:07,400
And it’s not your honor or glory, but “Isn’t God amazing?”

383
00:51:07,400 --> 00:51:13,619
“The God I believe in is amazing. He is your God too.”

384
00:51:13,619 --> 00:51:21,420
May this be a year when we can show that glory. Now, shall we go to communion?

385
00:51:22,480 --> 00:51:37,339
And in communion, let us each pray. Now, before communion today,

386
00:51:37,339 --> 00:51:51,780
Keichi, in your voice, please speak this Bible passage. “I received from the Lord what I also passed on to you:

387
00:51:51,780 --> 00:52:03,239
The Lord Jesus, on the night he was betrayed, took bread, and after giving thanks, broke it and said,

388
00:52:03,239 --> 00:52:12,960
‘This is my body, which is for you; do this in remembrance of me.’

389
00:52:12,960 --> 00:52:25,000
In the same way, after supper he took the cup and said, ‘This cup is the new covenant in my blood.

390
00:52:25,000 --> 00:52:33,699
Whenever you drink it, do this in remembrance of me.’ Therefore, whenever you eat this bread

391
00:52:33,699 --> 00:52:39,699
and drink this cup, you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes.”

392
00:52:39,699 --> 00:52:49,460
Whenever you eat this bread and drink this cup, you proclaim the Lord’s death until he comes.

393
00:52:49,460 --> 00:53:00,260
May what you express proclaim the Lord’s death and resurrection,

394
00:53:00,260 --> 00:53:09,440
so that each of you can become witnesses who proclaim it. Now, together with everyone,

395
00:53:09,440 --> 00:53:17,500
let us thank Jesus. Jesus, you broke your body for us. And

396
00:53:17,500 --> 00:53:26,519
you poured out the wine. This is truly the symbol of what you have done—making us new,

397
00:53:26,519 --> 00:53:38,440
re-creating us. Thank you. Let us partake.

398
00:53:38,440 --> 00:53:49,859
Jesus, you shed your blood and removed our sins from east to west.

399
00:53:49,859 --> 00:53:56,500
“It says, I will remember your sins no more.” Thank you for cleansing us with your blood,

400
00:53:56,500 --> 00:54:09,500
and making us new. Let us partake. Hallelujah, Heavenly Father,

401
00:54:09,500 --> 00:54:21,280
in January 2026, thank you that we can worship together in this gallery, Canon Chapel,

402
00:54:21,280 --> 00:54:27,920
and share communion together. “You will do a new thing.

403
00:54:27,920 --> 00:54:35,679
Do not remember the former things. Do not consider the things of old. Behold, I will do a new thing.

404
00:54:35,679 --> 00:54:42,260
Even now it is about to happen. Do you not know it? Indeed I will make a way in the wilderness,

405
00:54:42,260 --> 00:54:52,819
and rivers in the desert,” thank you for giving us your Word. The universal meaning of the word “new” we think of,

406
00:54:52,819 --> 00:55:03,159
and the word of your doing new things—thank you that we can taste it this year,

407
00:55:03,159 --> 00:55:11,760
and that this will be such a year. Thank you that what you give us is not hardship,

408
00:55:11,760 --> 00:55:18,799
but truly blessing. That each person here is precious, created by God,

409
00:55:18,799 --> 00:55:26,480
bearing God’s image. And each person is the salt of the earth and the light of the world,

410
00:55:26,480 --> 00:55:31,579
given the role to shine and reveal your glory. Thank you.

411
00:55:31,579 --> 00:55:38,940
Please be with each person’s creative work and the mission given to each person,

412
00:55:38,940 --> 00:55:46,159
and encourage them, and through the power of the Holy Spirit, through the centrifugal force of the Word,

413
00:55:46,159 --> 00:55:54,500
may your glory be revealed, and may inexplicable peace and joy be given to their hearts.

414
00:55:54,500 --> 00:56:01,199
May this be such a year. And through the people here, may Jesus’ grace be conveyed.

415
00:56:01,199 --> 00:56:10,380
May many people, and souls that are saved, vessels that follow Jesus, be raised up

416
00:56:10,380 --> 00:56:18,339
in this year. We pray in Jesus’ name. Amen. Thank you very much.
